* Builds on #162 

This PR adds a simple menu for being able to extend the site editor when it is used as an embedded application. In particular it adds support for adding menu items which do not exist in the core site editor framework. This is useful for instance when say we build a custom version of site editor which exports lines in a bespoke format. Additionally, while this approach increases boiler plate, it also allows plugins to be self contained. This will be useful when scaling site editor up and lead to more readable self contained code when adding new features.

The approach used is inspired by VSCode's extension API. At startup a plugin can declare its intent to register a new menu action. When it does so it will recieve an event handle the plugin should listen for. The plugin can then listen for the event handle separately thus enabling a complete integration with the UI of the site editor without much effort.

Here is a minimal example of how the new API may be used by a downstream application:
```rust
use bevy::prelude::*;
use librmf_site_editor::ui_command::TopLevelMenuExtensions;
use librmf_site_editor::ui_command::{EventHandle, MenuEvent};
use librmf_site_editor::SiteEditor;

#[derive(Debug, Default)]
struct MyMenuPlugin;

#[derive(Debug, Default, Resource)]
struct MyMenuHandler {
    event_handler: Option<EventHandle>,
}

/// Startup system to register menu.
fn init(mut data: ResMut<TopLevelMenuExtensions>, mut menu_handle: ResMut<MyMenuHandler>) {
    // This is all it takes to register a new menu item
    // We need to keep track of the event handler in order to make
    // sure that we can check the
    menu_handle.event_handler = Some(
        data.add_item(&"My Menu".to_string(), &"My Random Action".to_string())
            .unwrap(),
    );
}

/// Handler for menu item. All one needs tp dp is check that you recieve
/// an event that is of the same type as the one we are supposed to
/// handle.
fn watch_click(mut reader: EventReader<MenuEvent>, menu_handle: Res<MyMenuHandler>) {
    let Some(ref evt) = menu_handle.event_handler else {
        return;
    };
    for event in reader.iter() {
        if event.is_same(evt) {
            println!("Custom event clicked")
        }
    }
}

/// The actual plugin
impl Plugin for MyMenuPlugin {
    fn build(&self, app: &mut App) {
        app.init_resource::<MyMenuHandler>()
            .add_startup_system(init)
            .add_system(watch_click);
    }
}

/// Lets embed site editor in our application with our own plugin
fn main() {
    App::new()
        .add_plugin(SiteEditor)
        .add_plugin(MyMenuPlugin)
        .run();
}

```
This results in the following menu:
![image](https://github.com/open-rmf/rmf_site/assets/542272/3d224c37-70d1-4749-8962-7d3e211ac141)

Clicking on the menu will lead to a printout saying `Custom event clicked`.

Some things I had considered. It may not actually be necessary to issue a unique event handler, we could just listen for s string matching the menu item. However, I suspect this could lead to unwanted side effects where multiple plugins listen to an action that was only intended for one plugin, additionally listening for strings is more time conupming than just comparing a single integer.

Some things not considered by this:
* Order in which items appear: VS Code does provide a way of dealing with this.
* Sub-sub menus
* Visibility control
* Checkboxes and sliders

Many of these can be handled in a follow up PR.

